to reach my application.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.650
And I will commit my
change to the repository.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.260
I can now go check if my
configuration took effect

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.310
by refreshing.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:46.660
Great.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:49.540
I can now access the
Hipster Shop legacy CRM.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:53.050
Only this time, it's running on
an Anthos Kubernetes cluster.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.870
The network policy change that
I made in the Anthos Config

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.240
Management repository
was almost immediately

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:02.980
synced across my staging
and production clusters.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:07.000
After testing the Hipster Legacy
CRM in the staging environment,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.280
I can promote the
application to production

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.900
to begin taking advantage
of the rich feature

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.550
set of Anthos Service Mesh.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.440
Anthos Service Mesh
provides traffic management,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.140
observability, and
secure communication

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.890
across all the services
running on my Anthos clusters.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:25.610
From this single
pane of glass, I

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.940
am able to view the
10 micro services that

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.630
make up Hipster Shop's modern
online boutique website.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:34.450
But I'm also able to
see the Legacy CRM that

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.170
was just migrated from VMware.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.950
Anthos Service
Mesh automatically

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:41.950
proxies all requests
between services

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.180
and collects a
standard set of metrics

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:47.020
without having to make any
changes to the application

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:48.160
source code.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.890
I see those metrics
displayed in this table,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.830
and I will see them across many
of the other Anthos Service

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.220
Mesh screens, as these metrics
help gauge the performance

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.800
of my application services.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.100
Another helpful view
in Anthos Service Mesh

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:06.610
is the topology view, which,
again, shows the same services

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.260
that are running on Hipster
Shop's Anthos clusters,

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.200
but in a graphical
format that highlights

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.500
the inbound and outbound
connections of each service.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.770
And I can see here
the Hipster Legacy CRM

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:20.140
service that I just deployed.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:22.330
If I click on the
service, I will

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:25.180
see this set of standard
performance metrics displayed

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:27.460
in the widget on the right.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:29.770
Now as an operator,
my job requires

00:12:29.770 --> 00:12:32.200
that I keep all of
Hipster Shop's services

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.630
running across the staging
and production clusters

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.370
at an optimal state.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:38.930
You might ask, what
is an optimal state?

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:40.990
And the answer is that
it really depends.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:42.710
Each service is different.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.250
There may be some services
where slight degradation

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:47.920
in performance will negatively
impact the end user's

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:51.790
experience, while other
services may not be as delicate

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.890
and slight performance
changes will go unnoticed.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.190
With Anthos Service
Mesh, I have the ability

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:01.450
to set Service-Level Objectives,
or SLOs, for each service

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:04.540
so that I can proactively
monitor Hipster Shop's

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:07.510
applications' health and
ensure that end users are not

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:09.790
having a negative experience.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:13.630
I've already set up an SLO
Hipster Shop's legacy CRM

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.770
application.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:19.000
Let's take a closer look at
how I defined this objective.

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.800
The metric I'm interested in
monitoring and maintaining

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:31.670
for this service is latency, or
how long it takes the Hipster

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:34.550
Shop Legacy CRM
service to respond

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:35.690
when it receives a request.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.450
Next, I specify my
latency threshold.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:43.700
That is the maximum
amount of latency

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:46.480
I'm willing to accept for
my service to be considered

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.500
at an optimal state.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:53.290
I set the threshold
to be 20 milliseconds.

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:55.700
I then set the
time period I want

00:13:55.700 --> 00:14:00.590
to use for evaluating the SLO
to be a rolling one-day period.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.650
Finally, I choose
a performance goal.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:07.250
With my goal set to 90%, I am
saying that the Hipster Shop

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:09.920
Legacy CRM service
latency should

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:14.300
be below the threshold of 20
milliseconds 90% of the day.

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.510
So that wraps up our Java
application modernization demo

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:18.750
with Anthos.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.470
In this demo, you saw how
a Java Spring application

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:24.980
was migrated off of a virtual
machine running on VMware

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.960
to an Anthos Kubernetes cluster
running on Google Cloud's

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:29.660
Kubernetes Engine.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:33.010
You saw how a Kubernetes network
policy or any other company

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.090
good reason or
policy file can be

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:38.120
synced across multiple
clusters in a matter of seconds

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.310
using Anthos Config Management.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:43.520
And finally, you saw how an
operator can use Anthos Service

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.650
Mesh and the
observability metrics

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:48.380
it provides to set
service-level objectives

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:50.690
on their containerized services.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.700
Thank you, and we look
forward to showing you

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:54.860
more application
modernization tools

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.270
on Google Cloud in the future.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:59.020
